    {
      "image": "videos/04-open-loops/2.jpg",
      "seconds": 22.59,
      "text": "In the nineteen twenties, a young psychologist named Bluma Zeigarnik watched waiters in a Vienna restaurant. They could recall orders nobody had paid for in perfect detail. The moment the bill was settled, the order evaporated from memory. She tested it in the lab, interrupting people mid-task. They remembered the unfinished work far better than the work they completed.",
      "delivery": {
        "rate": 12,
        "pitch": 0
      },
